9. Requirements
ID
Area
Requirement
FR-001
Request handoff
After a successful pending void request, POS opens a clear in-terminal pending approval popup and displays the approval request identity.
FR-002
Reviewer queue
An authorized reviewer can open a POS queue containing pending pos_void requests with target, amount, location, requester, reason, and age.
FR-003
Decision
An authorized reviewer can approve or reject through the existing business-admin approval endpoints and may provide the existing bounded decision note.
FR-004
Result
The popup shows pending, approved/executed, rejected, unauthorized, stale/conflict, and unavailable states without claiming a mutation before server confirmation.
FR-005
Recovery
Refresh/retry is safe. The UI deduplicates by approval_request_id and never resubmits the void request merely because a decision request timed out.
BR-001
Pending semantics
A pending request leaves transaction and stock unchanged.
BR-002
Canonical execution
Only an approved pending pos_void request invokes the canonical POS void use case.
BR-003
Self-review
The requester cannot approve or reject their own request. UI disabling is secondary to backend rejection.
BR-004
Atomicity
Approval state and canonical void execution remain atomic and idempotent under concurrent reviewers and retries.
BR-005
Refund boundary
Void approval does not refund captured money; existing tender-specific refund workflows remain separate.
PERM-001
Reviewer authorization
Server enforcement requires Business Admin access, all-tenant scope, and business_admin:high_risk:approve, with existing master/admin exceptions.
SEC-001
Tenant isolation
The browser sends only an approval request ID and decision note. The server loads and locks the tenant-local row and verifies immutable target evidence.
SEC-002
Credential safety
No password, access token, refresh token, reviewer PIN, or tenant context is stored in localStorage/sessionStorage, logs, or the approval payload.
SEC-003
CSRF/session
Unsafe requests reuse the existing cookie-backed session, CSRF, refresh, and retry behavior.
UI-001
Accessible popup
Dialog focus, Escape handling, focus return, body-scroll locking, busy state, error announcement, and keyboard operation match existing POS dialog conventions.
UI-002
Responsive POS
The queue and decision dialog work at the repository's desktop, tablet, and mobile POS viewport gates without horizontal overflow.
AUD-001
Evidence
Existing request, decision, rejection, execution, actor, reason, note, and timestamps remain visible through the canonical audit path.
OPS-001
Temporary control
The POS-only surface can be disabled without changing pending request data or weakening the server approval contract.
REP-001
Production proof
The deployed POS asset must contain the new surface and be verified against the intended release SHA before enabling the workflow.

10. Implementation design
10.1 Backend and API
Do not create a new void approval endpoint. Reuse GET /business-admin/approvals?status=pending and POST /business-admin/approvals/:id/approve or /reject.
Keep apps/dgfy-api/src/modules/businessAdmin/usecases/businessAdminApprovalUseCase.js as the reviewer authorization boundary and apps/dgfy-api/src/modules/pos/usecases/posExceptionApprovalUseCases.js as the canonical decision/execution boundary.
Add or update transport tests only where the temporary surface exposes a missing contract: pending list filtering to pos_void, bounded note validation, 401/403 behavior, self-review denial, idempotent replay, and race/conflict behavior.
Before adding middleware, perform a live authenticated check of requireBusinessAdminDashboardEnabled. If the target tenant returns BUSINESS_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_DISABLED, use a tenant-scoped rollout configuration or add a narrowly scoped approval-only gate; never remove the dashboard gate from all other routes and never grant pos:void users reviewer authority.
If an approval-only gate is needed, document it as temporary configuration with an explicit removal condition in the same ADR amendment. The gate must still enforce existing tenant capability, reviewer scope, high-risk permission, self-review, and canonical action support.
10.2 Shared POS frontend
Extend packages/web-core/src/features/pos/hooks/usePosHistoryVoidWorkflow.js with pending approval state, reviewer queue loading, decision loading, selected request, and safe refresh behavior. Keep the existing transaction history and refund workflow state separate.
Add a thin packages/web-core/src/features/pos/services/posApprovalService.js wrapper around the existing API client. It may call the canonical /business-admin/approvals routes but must not contain authorization decisions or mutation logic.
Add a focused POSInlineVoidApprovalDialog component, or equivalent focused component, under packages/web-core/src/features/pos/components. It should render the request handoff, pending queue, decision note, decision result, and error states without importing apps/dgfy-pos BusinessAdminPage.jsx.
Extend packages/web-core/src/features/pos/components/POSTransactionHistoryPanel.jsx to replace `sent to Back Office' copy with accurate `awaiting manager approval' copy and expose the popup/queue entry point.
Thread an explicit inlineVoidApprovalEnabled prop from TerminalPageLayout through POSCheckoutTerminal. Enable it only on the existing standalone POS surface using VITE_APP_SURFACE === 'pos' and the temporary feature flag. Leave the IMS surface unchanged.
Derive reviewer affordances from terminalUser's current identity only as a display optimization. Match the backend policy for master/admin, business_admin:access, business_admin:view_all_branches, and business_admin:high_risk:approve. Treat every 401/403 as authoritative and remove or disable action controls after the response.
10.3 Session and handoff design
The popup must distinguish requester from reviewer using the server-returned requester identity or the current terminal user identity; it must not infer approval from possession of pos:void.
If the current user is not an eligible reviewer, show `Pending manager approval' and a safe handoff instruction. Do not present a fake approval button that will predictably fail.
If an authorized manager is already signed in, allow the queue to open in the same POS surface. If a manager must take over the terminal, use the existing account-switch/takeover boundary and preserve the active shift rules; do not implement a new credential modal in this phase.
Do not persist approval queue data as an offline mutation. If the network is unavailable, show the last known state only as stale and require a fresh server read before a decision.
10.4 Data and migration impact
No schema migration is expected. The existing business_admin_approval_requests table already supports pos_void, required reason, immutable payload, request hash, idempotency, status, decision actor, decision note, and execution timestamps. The implementation must prove this with existing migration/schema tests rather than adding a duplicate table or a client-owned approval status.
