Documentation and ADR checks: npm run lint:docs and npm run check:adr.
Browser verification: npm --prefix apps/dgfy-pos run test:e2e with the authenticated POS profile and the repository's desktop/tablet/mobile evidence procedure. Do not substitute a route screenshot for a proof that a decision was server-authorized and audited.
13. Security and failure handling
Condition
Required behavior
Current user is the requester
Show request details and pending state; disable Approve/Reject. If a decision is attempted, display the backend self-review error without changing state.
Current user has pos:void but not high-risk approval
They may request if existing guards permit, but cannot list or decide approvals. The popup must not imply that pos:void grants review authority.
Assigned-scope reviewer
Backend returns 403 because high-risk decisions require all scope. UI shows that tenant-wide review authority is required.
401 or expired session
Stop decision controls, use the existing cookie-backed refresh/session flow, and require a fresh server read. Never cache credentials or replay a stale decision automatically.
403 permission/tenant gate
Keep the request pending and show a safe operational message. Do not fall back to direct void or local approval.
409 stale target or another reviewer
Reload the approval row and transaction history. Treat the server result as final; do not retry blindly.
Decision request times out
Show unknown/pending, then refresh by approval request ID or queue. Do not submit a second approve call solely because the browser timed out.
Backend executes but UI closes
A later queue/history refresh must show executed/voided. Existing idempotent approval execution prevents duplicate transaction mutation.
Network offline
Disable decisions and display stale/unavailable state. Do not queue approval decisions as offline terminal operations.
Production bundle is stale
Do not claim the feature is live. Stop rollout, identify the deployed SHA/image, and follow release verification before enabling the flag.
Paid transaction
Void approval records the internal void only. Show a separate instruction for tender-specific refund handling; do not auto-refund.

14. Rollout, rollback, and removal
14.1 Release inventory
Included if changed: apps/dgfy-api route/middleware/tests; packages/web-core POS service, hook, dialog, panel, and tests; apps/dgfy-pos environment/route wiring, E2E, and build evidence; ADR 0086 amendment; phase ledger entry for Phase 353 when implementation is approved.
Excluded: apps/dgfy-ims, apps/dgfy-storefront, migration runner, payment/refund use cases, parked-sale cancellation behavior, and a new Back Office application.
The implementation PR must follow docs/ai/PR.md, use a Conventional Commit, target develop for ordinary work, scan changed files for DO NOT COMMIT markers, and group commits by domain if the change is split into logical batches.
14.2 Enablement
Land the implementation through the normal develop --> staging --> main promotion policy. Do not merge main from this planning task and do not treat a merged main commit as proof that production was deployed.
Verify the production deploy artifact and deployed SHA. The observed live stale bundle must be corrected before functional acceptance begins.
Run an authenticated smoke with a requester and a different all-scope reviewer. Verify the approval request, decision, canonical void, audit event, and UI result.
Enable the POS-only flag for the intended production surface/tenant. Keep reviewer authority unchanged and monitor 403, 409, decision-failure, and queue-refresh telemetry for the first release window.
14.3 Rollback
Preferred rollback is to disable the POS UI flag or redeploy the previous POS artifact. Pending approval rows remain safe and reviewable through the canonical API/Back Office when available; rollback must not delete requests, directly void transactions, or rewrite audit evidence. If a backend rollout gate or transport change was introduced, revert only that compatible change after confirming that existing pending requests remain readable and no committed approval is reversed.
14.4 Removal criteria
Dedicated Back Office #1354 approval workspace is deployed at the intended production host and passes live authenticated acceptance.
The Back Office path can list, approve, reject, audit, and recover the same pos_void requests without depending on the temporary popup.
Operations confirm that managers no longer need the POS reviewer surface, and the removal PR includes a migration/retention decision for no data because the canonical approval rows remain valid.
The POS flag is disabled, the inline components and service wrappers are removed in a follow-up phase, and ADR 0086 is amended again to close the temporary exception.
15. Acceptance criteria
A cashier with pos:void can submit a reasoned void request from POS History and receives a pending approval popup. The transaction and inventory are unchanged while pending.
An already authenticated all-scope high-risk reviewer can open the pending pos_void queue inside standalone POS and approve or reject without opening a Back Office route.
The requester cannot approve or reject the same request, even if the browser is tampered with or the decision endpoint is called directly.
Approval executes the existing canonical POS void use case exactly once, records the expected audit evidence, and returns an idempotent result on replay.
Rejection leaves the transaction and stock unchanged and is visible as rejected in the popup/queue.
The popup never treats a void as a refund and never triggers a tender-specific refund automatically.
Unauthorized, assigned-scope, expired-session, stale-target, timeout, and offline states fail closed with actionable UI copy.
The feature is visible only in the standalone POS surface and is disabled cleanly when the temporary flag is off.
Desktop, tablet, and mobile POS browser evidence passes, including focus, Escape, busy, error, and no-overflow checks.
Production evidence proves the deployed artifact contains this change and matches the intended release SHA; a stale bundle is a failed gate, not a pass.
No schema migration, duplicate approval owner, new credential store, or IMS surface is introduced.
16. Risks, assumptions, and explicit blockers
Item
Impact
Resolution
Production POS artifact is stale
The new popup cannot be observed even if code is correct.
Treat deployed asset/SHA verification as a hard Phase 353 gate and correct deployment through the release policy.
Business Admin rollout gate may be disabled
Existing approval endpoints may return BUSINESS_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_DISABLED.
Perform an authenticated target-tenant check. Use existing allowlist/configuration or design a narrow approval-only gate; never remove all route protection.
Same-terminal manager handoff is not fully specified
Changing the active session could affect shift ownership and terminal state.
First release supports an already authenticated reviewer and existing POS account-switch/takeover boundary only. Add a separate approved security design if a new in-popup login is required.
Shared web-core fan-out
A shared POS change can affect more than one app surface.
Gate with VITE_APP_SURFACE === 'pos', run web-core contract tests, and verify IMS does not receive the new reviewer UI.
Dirty worktree
Unrelated registration, Android build, generated artifact, and other user changes are present.
Do not stage, clean, reset, or overwrite them. Implementation must begin from a clean, accounted-for branch/worktree.
Temporary feature becomes permanent
A workaround can become a second long-term approval surface.
Record #1354 removal criteria, owner, and a follow-up phase in the ADR/issue before enablement.

